datory restart protocol was strong: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement and observed interruption, a changed boot ID, recovered timer and integrity service, unchanged database checksum, and a clean post-restart check. Lifecycle observations independently confirm the final boot identity and show no new sockets, failed services, or package-health damage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6000b5c601694772","cmd-e69e49114b524cf7","cmd-8b4ca33ca41c4815","cmd-b3c5e67af1084e36","cmd-5a7506e3ad8a45e1","cmd-b9cdbff6b20641b2","cmd-efa8ab04a944498c","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 has a protected, functional /etc integrity baseline that directly detected and reported a controlled change, survived the required restart, and retained the original RHEL release and kernel. Evidence quality is high; hygiene is reduced slightly by one abandoned temporary output file.","limitations":["The behavioral probe tested an added file rather than modifying or removing an existing file. Detection of existing-file content changes is supported by the configured SHA-512 rule but was not separately exercised.","Lifecycle snapshots provide bounded inventories rather than a complete filesystem diff; the captured command timeline nevertheless documents the task-related persistent artifacts."]}
